Post-Viral Autonomic and Cardiovascular Dysregulation
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Profound orthostatic intolerance/dizziness, Exaggerated tachycardia upon standing, Extreme post-viral fatigue, Abnormal tilt-table test

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-038
Reason for Visit: Abrupt onset of severe dizziness, unprovoked resting palpitations, and frequent presyncope requiring urgent assessment to rule out primary cardiac arrhythmia or structural heart disease.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing severe lightheadedness and a massively exaggerated heart rate upon standing, leading to near-fainting episodes. Urgent physical assessment is required to evaluate haemodynamic stability, rule out critical arrhythmias, and assess volume status prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Every time I stand up, my heart hammers in my chest, my vision goes black, and my legs turn purple."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a complete onset of symptoms distinctly following a recently resolved acute viral illness (severe upper respiratory infection) 4 weeks ago. The patient describes profound, distinct, and extreme orthostatic intolerance directly triggered by assuming an upright posture. Over the last 3 days, this has escalated to sudden, inappropriate resting tachycardia and severe dizziness, intense lightheadedness, and frequent presyncope. The patient notes severe, unusual, and unprovoked palpitations occurring entirely at rest. There is extreme, debilitating generalized fatigue that dramatically worsens with upright posture, alongside distinct pooling of blood in the lower extremities presenting as a purplish-red discoloration (acrocyanosis) upon standing.
Functional Status: Bedbound, highly fearful of standing due to the immediate presyncope.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; extremely high risk of a syncopal fall resulting in trauma.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/70 (Supine) -> 105/65 (Standing) | HR: 78 (Supine) -> 125 (Standing) | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Fatigued, anxious. When standing for assessment, patient becomes visibly pale, diaphoretic, and requests to lie down immediately.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals regular rhythm, normal heart sounds, no murmurs. Lungs are clear. Active orthostatic vital sign testing reveals a massive, sustained heart rate increment (increase of 47 bpm) within 3 minutes of standing, accompanied by severe symptom reproduction and acrocyanosis of the feet, without a severe drop in blood pressure. Neurological exam is grossly intact when supine. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Profound orthostatic intolerance/dizziness, Exaggerated tachycardia upon standing, Extreme post-viral fatigue, and Abnormal tilt-table test (clinical orthostatic test positive).
Environmental Observations: Patient has been crawling to the bathroom to avoid standing.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Denies illicit drug or extreme caffeine use.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Post-Viral Autonomic and Cardiovascular Dysregulation (POTS-like syndrome) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Primary Arrhythmia (e.g., SVT) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The tachycardia is entirely and predictably postural (resolves upon lying flat) rather than a sudden, unprovoked paroxysmal electrical circuit issue.

Orthostatic Hypotension (Volume Depletion) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The lack of a precipitous drop in blood pressure (>20mmHg systolic) upon standing, whereas the heart rate massive jump is the primary physiological anomaly.

Post-Viral Myocarditis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal resting supine heart rate, absence of chest pain, clear lung bases, and lack of a pathological murmur.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Severe fall risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe, debilitating post-viral orthostatic intolerance characterized by a dramatic postural tachycardia. The frequent near-fainting episodes and the inability to safely ambulate to the bathroom created an immediate safety crisis. An urgent on-site clinical assessment was strictly required to perform supervised orthostatic vital signs, confidently rule out a life-threatening primary cardiac arrhythmia or acute myocarditis, and immediately initiate mechanical and volume-expanding interventions to prevent a catastrophic syncopal fall. This intervention safely manages an acute functional decline, strictly satisfying the urgent after-hours criteria (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advise immediate counter-maneuvers (e.g., crossing legs and tensing core muscles before standing). Prescribe aggressive fluid loading (2.5-3 Litres daily) and high sodium intake to maximize intravascular volume. Urgent referral for a formal 12-lead ECG, Holter monitor, and formal tilt-table testing via the regular GP to rule out structural pathology and confirm the diagnosis.
Positive Behavioral Change: By systematically drinking 500mL of cold water and putting on tight compression stockings immediately before getting out of bed, the patient will physically force blood out of the legs and into the central circulation; avoiding the terrifying black-out sensation will strongly reinforce this strict morning mechanical routine.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Waist-high medical-grade compression tights (20-30mmHg).
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48-72 hours to review ECG and consider pharmacological therapy (e.g., beta-blockers, fludrocortisone).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if the patient experiences a complete loss of consciousness, if the heart races uncontrollably while lying completely flat, or if severe, crushing chest pain develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; structural heart disease must always be excluded by an ECG.
Clinical Course: The viral infection temporarily damaged the autonomic nerves controlling your blood vessels. Recovery is often slow and highly fluctuating, requiring strict lifestyle modifications to manage symptoms.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Your body is currently unable to pump blood to your brain effectively when you stand. Do not stand up quickly under any circumstances. If you feel dizzy, drop to the floor immediately to avoid hitting your head.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: When you stand, gravity pulls your blood into your legs. Normally, your nerves squeeze the blood vessels to push it back up. Your post-viral nerves are failing to squeeze, so your heart goes into overdrive trying to compensate. Salt, water, and compression tights act as a physical cast for your blood vessels.
Actionable Steps: Sit on the edge of the bed and pump your calves for 2 minutes before standing. Add salt generously to all meals. Avoid hot showers, which dilate your blood vessels and worsen the blood pooling.

